/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Books & Language (batch z) */
WB.L("alice-in-wonderland", "literature", "beginner", "Alice in Wonderland: the story told on a river",
  "On a summer afternoon in 1862, a shy maths lecturer made up a story to entertain three bored girls in a rowing boat. It became one of the most translated books ever written.",
  [
    ["A story made up aloud", "Alice began as a tale improvised on a rowing trip in 1862 for a ten-year-old girl who asked for it to be written down.",
      "On 4 July 1862, Charles Dodgson, a mathematics lecturer at Christ Church, Oxford, rowed up the Thames towards Godstow. With him were his friend Robinson Duckworth and the three daughters of the college dean, Henry Liddell: Lorina, Alice and Edith. Alice was ten.\n\nTo pass the time, Dodgson spun a tale about a girl named Alice who tumbles down a rabbit hole into a world where nothing behaves sensibly. Duckworth later remembered asking if he was inventing it on the spot, and Dodgson said yes. At the end of the day, Alice Liddell asked him to write it down for her. He took the request seriously, though it took him more than two years to finish."],
    ["From gift to book", "A handwritten gift grew into a published novel, and the author invented the pen name Lewis Carroll.",
      "Dodgson wrote the story out by hand, added his own pen drawings, and in November 1864 gave Alice a small volume titled Alice's Adventures Under Ground. Friends who read it, including the writer George MacDonald, whose children loved it, urged him to publish.\n\nHe expanded the tale, adding episodes such as the Mad Tea-Party and the Cheshire Cat. He also built a pen name from his own: Charles Lutwidge became Carolus Ludovicus, then was reversed and anglicised into Lewis Carroll.\n\nHe hired John Tenniel, the leading cartoonist of Punch, and paid for the illustrations himself. Alice's Adventures in Wonderland appeared from Macmillan in 1865, and Tenniel's drawings have shaped how readers picture Alice ever since."],
    ["The edition that was recalled", "The first print run was withdrawn over poor picture quality, which made the few surviving copies enormously valuable.",
      "Tenniel was unhappy with the first printing of 2,000 copies in 1865, complaining that the pictures were badly printed. Dodgson agreed to withdraw the edition and pay for a new one. The reprint, dated 1866, is the edition most Victorian readers knew.\n\nMany unbound sheets from the rejected run were sold to an American publisher and issued in the United States. The few bound copies already given away became extreme rarities. Only around two dozen copies of the original 1865 edition are known to survive, mostly in libraries, and one can sell for well over a million dollars at auction.\n\nA perfectionist's fussing over ink and paper created one of the most sought-after books in the world."],
    ["Nonsense with rules", "Wonderland's absurdity is built from logic pushed to extremes, along with parody and wordplay, and the Mad Hatter is not named that in the book.",
      "Wonderland feels chaotic, but much of its humour is logic pushed to ridiculous extremes, as you might expect from a mathematician. Characters take words literally, argue over definitions and follow rules that make no sense. Many of Alice's poems parody the moralising verses Victorian children had to memorise.\n\nThe Hatter asks why a raven is like a writing desk and admits he has no idea. For years Carroll gave no answer, and only in a later edition did he suggest a playful one.\n\nThe book never actually calls him the Mad Hatter. He is simply the Hatter, though the Cheshire Cat assures Alice that everyone there is mad. The famous nickname came from readers and later adaptations."],
    ["Myths and a manuscript's return", "Popular stories about Queen Victoria and mercury-poisoned hatters are unproven or false, while the original manuscript did return to Britain as a gift.",
      "One legend says Queen Victoria loved Alice and asked for Carroll's next book, receiving a dry mathematical treatise. Carroll publicly denied this in 1896. Another claim says the Hatter was inspired by hat makers poisoned by the mercury used on felt. That poisoning was real, and the phrase mad as a hatter predates the book, but there is no evidence Carroll was making that point. His family's relationship with the Liddells cooled in 1863 for reasons nobody fully knows, because the relevant diary pages were later cut out.\n\nIn 1928 Alice, by then Mrs Hargreaves, sold the manuscript at auction in London, and it went to the United States. In 1948 American donors led by Luther Evans, the Librarian of Congress, bought and returned it to Britain in thanks for its wartime role. It is now in the British Library."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did the story of Alice first come into being?", ["It was drafted as a school lesson","It was commissioned by a publisher","It was improvised aloud on a rowing trip","It was adapted from an older folk tale"], 2, "Dodgson invented it on the spot for the Liddell girls, and only wrote it down at Alice's request."],
    [1, "How was the pen name Lewis Carroll created?", ["It was the name of an Oxford colleague","It was chosen by the publisher","His name was Latinised, then reversed","It came from a character in the story"], 2, "Charles Lutwidge became Carolus Ludovicus and then Lewis Carroll."],
    [2, "Why are surviving copies of the 1865 edition so valuable?", ["The print run was recalled, so few survive","It contains handwritten notes by Carroll","It was printed in a signed limited run","It was the only edition with colour plates"], 0, "Because the run was recalled over print quality, only around two dozen copies are known."],
    [3, "What is true of the Mad Hatter in the book?", ["He is named the Mad Hatter on his first appearance","He solves the raven riddle in the story","He is based on a real Oxford hat maker","He is only ever called the Hatter"], 3, "The nickname Mad Hatter came from readers and adaptations, not from the text."],
    [4, "What is the evidence that the Hatter was inspired by mercury poisoning?", ["Carroll wrote about it in his diary","None; the link is only speculation","Tenniel said he drew it from a patient","The Liddell family confirmed it"], 1, "Mercury poisoning in hat making was real, but nothing shows Carroll meant to refer to it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A rowing trip, a ten-year-old and an invented story. Why one copy fetches millions."});

WB.L("ern-malley-hoax", "literature", "beginner", "Ern Malley: the poet who never lived",
  "In 1944 an Australian magazine hailed the discovery of a brilliant dead poet. He had been invented in a single afternoon by two bored soldiers.",
  [
    ["Modernism under attack", "In the 1940s Australian modernists around Angry Penguins argued for a break with old forms, and traditionalist critics thought the new poetry was nonsense.",
      "In the early 1940s, Australian art and literature were split between traditionalists and a younger crowd excited by modernism, the experimental style of writers such as T. S. Eliot and painters like Picasso.\n\nAt the centre of the avant-garde was Angry Penguins, a magazine co-founded in Adelaide in 1940 by Max Harris, who was still a teenager. Melbourne patrons John and Sunday Reed backed it, and their circle included the painters Sidney Nolan and Albert Tucker. It published surrealist-influenced poetry and argued that Australia needed to break with old forms.\n\nTo its critics, much of this poetry looked like pretentious nonsense that anyone could produce. Two of them decided to test that idea."],
    ["An afternoon's invention", "James McAuley and Harold Stewart stitched random phrases into poems and invented a dead poet to sign them.",
      "James McAuley and Harold Stewart were young poets who disliked modernism and preferred traditional craft. In 1943 both were serving in the army in Melbourne. By their own account, they wrote a whole body of work in a single afternoon.\n\nThey opened books at random, including a dictionary, a collected Shakespeare, a dictionary of quotations and an American report on draining swamps to control mosquitoes, and wove the phrases into sixteen poems. They deliberately included nonsense, false allusions and clumsy lines.\n\nThen they invented an author: Ern Malley, a mechanic and insurance salesman who had supposedly died young in 1943, leaving his poems unpublished. They titled the collection The Darkening Ecliptic."],
    ["The bait is taken", "Max Harris believed he had found a lost genius and devoted a special issue to him, until the press exposed the hoax.",
      "The poems reached Harris with a covering letter from Ethel Malley, Ern's supposedly grieving sister, who said she did not understand them but wondered if they were any good.\n\nHarris was thrilled. He believed he had found a major poet who had died unrecognised, and in 1944 he devoted a special issue of Angry Penguins to Ern Malley, with a cover painting by Sidney Nolan inspired by the poems. Some readers suspected a joke from the start.\n\nWithin weeks, journalists began investigating who Ethel and Ern really were. In June 1944 the press revealed the truth, and McAuley and Stewart explained their experiment, declaring that the poems were deliberately meaningless."],
    ["Ridicule and a courtroom", "The hoax made Harris a national laughing stock and led to an obscenity conviction that seemed to prove the hoaxers right.",
      "The story was a sensation, and newspapers across the country mocked Harris and modernism together. Worse followed. South Australian police charged Harris with publishing indecent material, pointing to lines in the Ern Malley poems and other contributions to the magazine.\n\nThe trial in Adelaide in 1944 became famous for its absurdity. A police detective was asked to explain why certain words and images were immoral, and admitted he did not know what some of them meant. Harris was convicted and fined five pounds.\n\nAngry Penguins survived only a few more years. For a while the episode seemed to prove what the hoaxers intended: that modernist poetry could not tell genuine art from random words."],
    ["The poet who outlived them", "Many readers later judged the fake poems genuinely good, which raises lasting questions about authorship, intention and meaning.",
      "Then something unexpected happened. Over the following decades, many critics and poets argued that the poems were striking, with vivid images and an odd emotional power, however they were made. Sidney Nolan returned to Ern Malley in his art for years, and the American poet John Ashbery was among the admirers. Some suggested the hoaxers had, despite themselves, poured real talent and feeling into the work. The poems have stayed in print ever since, and the hoax inspired later writing, including Peter Carey's 2003 novel My Life as a Fake.\n\nDoes a poem's value depend on what its author intended? Meaning kept appearing where the authors said there was none. Some historians argue the hoax set Australian modernism back by years. Max Harris went on to a long career as a bookseller, publisher and columnist, and never fully disowned Ern."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the hoaxers believe about modernist poetry?", ["That it was too traditional for Australia's readers","That it copied older poets far too closely","That it was pretentious nonsense anyone could write","That it was too costly for small magazines to publish"], 2, "The hoaxers doubted modernists could tell real poetry from nonsense, and built the hoax to test that."],
    [1, "How were the Ern Malley poems actually made?", ["By weaving together phrases from random books","By copying the notes of a real poet who had died","By translating poems from French into English","By reworking the early verse of Max Harris"], 0, "The poems were stitched from random phrases and deliberately included nonsense."],
    [2, "Why did Max Harris publish Ern Malley so lavishly?", ["He had been told it was a joke","He wanted to expose a rival magazine","He was paid by the Malley family","He thought he had found a lost genius"], 3, "Harris was convinced he had discovered a lost genius, so he dedicated a special issue to him."],
    [3, "What happened to Harris after the hoax was revealed?", ["He was banned from publishing for life","He was prosecuted for indecency and fined","He was sued by the hoaxers","He closed the magazine immediately"], 1, "A South Australian court convicted him of publishing indecent material and fined him five pounds."],
    [4, "Why does the Ern Malley affair still interest critics?", ["It proved modernism had no value","It showed the poems were plagiarised","Poems meant as nonsense won admirers","It uncovered a true dead poet"], 2, "The poems' lasting appeal raises the question of whether intention or reading gives a work its value."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two soldiers wrote a famous poet in one afternoon. The poems outlived the joke."});

WB.L("pen-names", "literature", "beginner", "Pen names: why writers hide",
  "George Orwell, Mark Twain and George Eliot were not real names. Behind every pen name is a reason, from prejudice to privacy to a bit of mischief.",
  [
    ["Not who they seemed", "A pen name is an invented name an author publishes under, and writers have long used them for reasons from prejudice to privacy.",
      "A pen name, also called a pseudonym or nom de plume, is a name an author publishes under instead of their own. The practice is ancient and widespread. Voltaire was born François-Marie Arouet. Lewis Carroll was Charles Dodgson. George Orwell was Eric Arthur Blair. Australian readers know Henry Handel Richardson, author of The Fortunes of Richard Mahony, but the writer was in fact Ethel Florence Lindesay Richardson.\n\nWriters have adopted pen names to escape prejudice, to protect their families, to keep a day job, to separate different kinds of writing, or simply because they liked the sound of something better. Each disguise tells a small story about the pressures writers faced in their time."],
    ["Bells and Eliot", "The Brontë sisters and Mary Ann Evans chose male-sounding names so their books would be judged seriously.",
      "In 1846 three sisters in a Yorkshire parsonage published poems as Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell. In 1847 Jane Eyre by Currer Bell became a sensation, and Wuthering Heights and Agnes Grey followed. The authors were Charlotte, Emily and Anne Brontë. They chose names that were not obviously female because, Charlotte later explained, critics treated women writers with prejudice. Charlotte and Anne revealed themselves to their publisher in 1848.\n\nMary Ann Evans, a respected editor and translator, turned to fiction in the 1850s as George Eliot. She wanted her work judged as serious literature, not light romance. She also lived openly with the married writer George Henry Lewes, a scandal in Victorian England. A male name kept attention on the books. Her identity soon leaked, but she kept the name."],
    ["Twain and Orwell", "Some pen names carry hidden meanings from the writer's life, such as a riverboat depth call or a Suffolk river.",
      "Samuel Clemens worked as a Mississippi riverboat pilot before he became a writer. On the river, crew called out depth readings, and mark twain meant the second mark on the line: two fathoms, about 3.7 metres, deep enough for a steamboat to pass safely. Clemens began signing his newspaper writing Mark Twain in 1863.\n\nEric Blair adopted the name George Orwell in 1933 for his first book, Down and Out in Paris and London, partly to spare his family embarrassment about his time living in poverty. George was a solidly English name, and the River Orwell in Suffolk was one he knew.\n\nBoth disguises became so famous that the real names now sound like the pseudonyms."],
    ["Starting over in secret", "Established authors sometimes take new names to escape their own fame, and a leak can transform a book's sales overnight.",
      "Pen names are not only for beginners. Stephen King published several novels as Richard Bachman, partly because publishers then believed more than about one book a year by the same author would saturate the market. A bookshop worker noticed similarities and researched copyright records, and King was exposed in 1985.\n\nJ. K. Rowling's initials were themselves a kind of disguise. Her publisher suggested boys might be reluctant to read a book by a woman, and she borrowed the K from her grandmother Kathleen. In 2013 she published a crime novel, The Cuckoo's Calling, as Robert Galbraith. It had modest sales until her authorship leaked, after which it shot up the bestseller lists overnight.\n\nThe Galbraith episode suggests that names strongly influence sales, if not quality."],
    ["The writer behind the curtain", "Some modern authors use pen names to keep the focus on the work, or so loyal readers know what to expect.",
      "The Italian novelist who writes as Elena Ferrante has kept her real identity officially private since her first novel in 1992, even as her Neapolitan novels became international bestsellers. She has said that books, once written, do not need their authors. Journalists have tried to unmask her, prompting fierce debate about whether readers have any right to know.\n\nPen names raise interesting questions. Does knowing an author's gender, background or reputation change how we read?\n\nToday, pen names are also common in genre fiction, where authors may write romance under one name and thrillers under another so that loyal readers know what to expect."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why have writers adopted pen names?", ["Because the law requires a registered name","Because publishers always insist on it","To avoid prejudice or protect privacy","Only to sound more impressive"], 2, "Writers have used pseudonyms for many reasons, from prejudice and privacy to keeping a day job."],
    [1, "Why did Mary Ann Evans publish as George Eliot?", ["Because a male name was legally required","To hide that she was a translator","To be taken seriously and protect her privacy","Because her publisher invented it"], 2, "A male name stopped her being typecast as a romance writer and kept attention off her unconventional life."],
    [2, "What does the name Mark Twain refer to?", ["A riverboat depth call meaning two fathoms","A character from his first novel","A mining term from the goldfields","A nickname given by his publisher"], 0, "On the Mississippi, mark twain meant two fathoms, deep enough for a steamboat."],
    [3, "What happened to The Cuckoo's Calling when Rowling's authorship leaked?", ["It was withdrawn from sale","It sold the same as before","The publisher cancelled the series","Its sales rose sharply overnight"], 3, "The book had modest sales as Robert Galbraith, then shot up the bestseller lists once the author was known."],
    [4, "Why do some genre authors use several pen names?", ["Because tax rules require a new name","So loyal readers know what to expect","To avoid competing with their publisher","Because bookshops require separate names"], 1, "A name signals a style, so a writer of romance and thrillers can keep each audience's expectations clear."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Her name on the cover could have cost her readers. Why writers choose a disguise."});

WB.L("dracula-bram-stoker", "literature", "beginner", "Dracula: the theatre manager's monster",
  "The world's most famous vampire was created by a busy Irish theatre manager who never set foot in Transylvania. Much of what we think we know about Dracula isn't in the book.",
  [
    ["The theatre manager", "Bram Stoker spent most of his career managing a London theatre and wrote fiction in his spare time.",
      "Abraham 'Bram' Stoker was born in Dublin in 1847. As a small child he was so ill that he could barely walk, and he later wrote that he spent his early years in bed listening to his mother's stories, including grim memories of a cholera epidemic. He recovered completely and became a champion athlete at Trinity College Dublin.\n\nHe worked as a civil servant at Dublin Castle while writing theatre reviews for free. One review praised the actor Henry Irving, the two became friends, and in 1878 Stoker moved to London to become business manager of Irving's Lyceum Theatre. He held the job for around 27 years, handling everything from finances to tours, and wrote fiction in the gaps."],
    ["Vampires before Dracula", "Stoker built on a century of vampire folklore and fiction and added scale, research and method.",
      "Vampires did not begin with Stoker. Eastern European folklore told of the restless dead returning to harm the living, and vampire panics in the 1700s spread across Europe. In 1819 John Polidori's story The Vampyre gave the creature an aristocratic, seductive character. In the 1840s the cheap serial Varney the Vampire thrilled readers for years. In 1872 Sheridan Le Fanu published Carmilla, about a female vampire, which scholars widely see as an influence on Stoker.\n\nWhat Stoker added was scale and method. He spent several years researching and planning, filling notebooks with ideas, and fused folklore, modern technology and Victorian anxieties into one sweeping thriller that would overshadow everything before it."],
    ["A name found in Whitby", "Stoker's villain began as Count Wampyr and took the name Dracula from a library book he found in Whitby.",
      "Stoker's surviving working notes show his vampire began as Count Wampyr. In 1890 he holidayed in Whitby, a Yorkshire fishing town with a ruined abbey on the cliff. In the local library he read an 1820 book about Wallachia and Moldavia that mentioned a ruler called Dracula and claimed the word meant devil in the local language. Stoker seized the name.\n\nThe historical figure behind it was Vlad III of Wallachia, known as Vlad the Impaler, but scholars point out that Stoker seems to have known very little about him beyond the name.\n\nWhitby itself found its way into the novel. Dracula comes ashore there when a ship runs aground in a storm, and he leaps onto land in the shape of a large dog."],
    ["A thriller told in documents", "Dracula is told through diaries, letters and recordings, and its heroes beat an ancient monster with modern technology and teamwork.",
      "Dracula, published in May 1897, has no single narrator. It is assembled from journals, letters, newspaper cuttings, a ship's log and telegrams. This form, called an epistolary novel, makes the story feel like a case file of real evidence.\n\nIt was also strikingly modern. Dr Seward records his diary on a phonograph, and Mina Harker types up and organises the documents on a typewriter, cross-referencing them to track the Count. The heroes use trains, telegrams and blood transfusions.\n\nStoker set an ancient monster against the newest technology of the 1890s, and much of the tension comes from that clash. The heroes win less through strength than through information, teamwork and careful paperwork."],
    ["Myths and a stolen film", "Several famous vampire rules come from films, not the novel, and Florence Stoker's lawsuit nearly destroyed the unauthorised Nosferatu.",
      "Readers who come to the novel from films are often surprised. Stoker's Count walks around London in daylight; the sun weakens his powers but does not destroy him. Sunlight as a killer was popularised by the 1922 German silent film Nosferatu. The novel's Count is first an old man with a long white moustache who grows younger as he feeds. The suave figure in evening dress owes much to stage adaptations and Bela Lugosi's 1931 Hollywood performance. Garlic, crucifixes and a stake through the heart do appear, but Stoker freely mixed his rules.\n\nStoker died in 1912 with modest means. Nosferatu was an unauthorised adaptation that renamed the Count as Orlok. His widow Florence sued, and a German court ordered copies destroyed. Some prints survived abroad, and the film is now considered a masterpiece."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Bram Stoker's main career?", ["Full-time novelist and magazine writer","Lecturer at Trinity College Dublin","Business manager of a London theatre","Newspaper editor in his home city"], 2, "Stoker managed Henry Irving's Lyceum Theatre for around 27 years and wrote fiction in spare moments."],
    [1, "What did Stoker add to earlier vampire stories?", ["The first ever female vampire character","The idea of vampires as aristocrats","Years of research and careful planning","A factual account of the vampire panics"], 2, "Others had written about vampires, but Stoker planned and fused folklore and modern concerns at scale."],
    [2, "Where did the name Dracula come from?", ["A library book he read in Whitby","A Romanian friend of Irving","A real prince he visited in Wallachia","His mother's childhood stories"], 0, "Stoker's notes began with Count Wampyr, and he took Dracula from a book he found in Whitby."],
    [3, "How do the characters defeat Dracula in the novel?", ["By physical strength and combat alone","By relying only on old folklore","By avoiding modern technology entirely","Information, teamwork and careful records"], 3, "The heroes use phonographs, typewriters, trains and telegrams to organise and share what they know."],
    [4, "Which popular idea about vampires is not in Stoker's novel?", ["A stake through the heart can kill them","Sunlight destroys them","Garlic can ward them off","They can appear as a dog, a bat or a wolf"], 1, "In the book the Count walks about in daylight, and the sunlight kill came from Nosferatu."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The most famous vampire never burned in sunlight in his own book."});

WB.L("charles-dickens", "literature", "beginner", "Charles Dickens and the cliffhanger",
  "Dickens wrote his novels a few chapters at a time, while readers waited for each new instalment. A secret childhood trauma shaped much of what he wrote.",
  [
    ["The boy in the factory", "A painful childhood spent pasting labels in a warehouse gave Dickens a lifelong sympathy for poor children.",
      "Charles Dickens was born in Portsmouth in 1812, the son of a navy pay clerk who was hopeless with money. In 1824, when Charles was twelve, his father was imprisoned for debt in London's Marshalsea prison, and much of the family moved in with him, as was common then.\n\nCharles was sent to work in a warehouse near the Thames, pasting labels onto pots of boot blacking for about ten hours a day. The job lasted perhaps a year, but he felt abandoned and humiliated. He told almost no one. The full story became public only after his death, when his friend John Forster published his biography.\n\nThe echoes run through his novels, which are full of orphans, debtors and cruel institutions."],
    ["Reporter to Pickwick", "Dickens's training as a shorthand reporter sharpened his ear, and a comic serial made him Britain's most popular author in his mid-twenties.",
      "Dickens had little formal schooling, but he taught himself shorthand and became a fast, skilled reporter, including in the press gallery of parliament. He watched people closely and listened to how they spoke. His humorous sketches of London life appeared under the pen name Boz.\n\nIn 1836 a publisher asked him to write text to accompany a series of comic sporting illustrations. Dickens took control of the project, which became The Pickwick Papers. Sales started slowly, but when the witty Cockney servant Sam Weller appeared, the story took off. By the end of its run, each monthly part was selling tens of thousands of copies. Dickens was in his mid-twenties and already the most popular author in Britain."],
    ["The serial machine", "Publishing in cheap instalments let Dickens write a few chapters ahead, end on suspense and respond to readers.",
      "Most of Dickens's novels first appeared in parts rather than as complete books. The typical format was a monthly instalment of about 32 pages with two illustrations, costing one shilling, usually running for nineteen or twenty months. Some novels, such as Oliver Twist, ran in magazines.\n\nThis made fiction affordable for more people, since buying a part a month was easier than paying for a whole book. It also shaped how Dickens wrote. He often stayed only a few instalments ahead of publication, ended parts on moments of suspense and could adjust stories in response to readers' reactions.\n\nFamilies and neighbours gathered to read new instalments aloud, so a single copy might reach many listeners."],
    ["Little Nell and Christmas", "Readers grieved openly for Dickens's characters, and A Christmas Carol helped shape the modern idea of Christmas while piracy cost him income.",
      "The Old Curiosity Shop, published weekly in 1840 and 1841, followed the doomed child Little Nell. As her death approached, readers begged Dickens to spare her. A famous story says crowds on a New York wharf shouted to arriving ships asking whether she was dead, but historians note the tale is poorly documented and may be embellished. What is certain is that many readers grieved openly.\n\nIn 1843, short of money, Dickens wrote A Christmas Carol in about six weeks. He insisted on an expensive edition with gilt edges and colour illustrations at a low price, so his profits disappointed. The story helped popularise a Christmas of family, generosity and feasting.\n\nHe also fought piracy. American publishers could reprint British books without paying the author, and the United States did not protect foreign authors meaningfully until 1891."],
    ["Readings and Staplehurst", "Dickens became a star performer on paid reading tours, which earned a fortune but exhausted him.",
      "From the late 1850s, Dickens gave paid public readings of his work, playing every character with different voices and gestures. Audiences in Britain and America packed halls to see him. The tours earned him a fortune but exhausted him, especially his violent reading of the murder of Nancy from Oliver Twist.\n\nIn June 1865 he survived a serious railway accident at Staplehurst in Kent, when his train crashed on a bridge under repair. He helped tend the injured and dying, then remembered his manuscript and climbed back into the carriage to retrieve it. He was shaken for the rest of his life.\n\nHe died in 1870, five years to the day after the crash, leaving The Mystery of Edwin Drood unfinished."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Dickens's childhood shape his novels?", ["He wrote only about wealthy families and clergy","His long schooling gave him a classical style","Child labour after his father's imprisonment","His father's navy career inspired his plots"], 2, "His humiliating factory job left a lasting sympathy for orphans, debtors and the poor."],
    [1, "What skill from reporting helped make Dickens a writer?", ["Listening closely to how people spoke","Writing very long and detailed reports","Sketching the scenes he reported on","Translating news from foreign papers"], 0, "Years as a shorthand reporter trained him to watch and listen to people closely."],
    [2, "Why did serial publication change how Dickens wrote?", ["He finished every novel before publishing it","He wrote only short stories for magazines","He ignored reader reactions as he wrote","He wrote as he went, ending parts on suspense"], 3, "Writing as he went let him build suspense and adjust stories in response to readers."],
    [3, "Why did A Christmas Carol disappoint Dickens financially?", ["Most readers waited for a cheaper edition","Its lavish production cost left little profit","He sold the rights before it was published","The printer failed to deliver the books on time"], 1, "His insistence on an expensive-looking edition at a low price squeezed his profits."],
    [4, "What did Dickens's reading tours cost him?", ["They made him a financial loss overall","They ended his career as a novelist","They earned a fortune but left him exhausted","They were only ever held at home in London"], 2, "Performing every character was draining, especially the murder of Nancy."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"He pasted labels on boot polish at twelve and never told anyone. It shaped his novels."});

WB.L("invented-languages", "literature", "intermediate", "Invented languages: from Esperanto to Klingon",
  "People have built entire languages from scratch, hoping to unite the world, deepen a fantasy, or just for fun. Most failed. A few took on lives of their own.",
  [
    ["A very old hobby", "People have invented languages for nearly nine centuries, for logic, communication or fiction.",
      "Constructed languages, often called conlangs, are designed by a person rather than evolving naturally in a community. One of the earliest recorded is the Lingua Ignota, or unknown language, created by the German abbess Hildegard of Bingen in the twelfth century, with around a thousand invented words.\n\nIn the 1600s, philosophers such as John Wilkins in England tried to build perfectly logical languages, where every word's form would reveal its meaning by sorting all of reality into categories. These schemes were brilliant and unusable.\n\nHistorians have counted hundreds of attempts. Their makers have had three main motives: to help people from different nations communicate, to explore ideas about logic and thought, or to bring a fictional world to life."],
    ["Volapük, then Esperanto", "Volapük boomed and split because its creator held on to control, and the simpler Esperanto took over.",
      "In 1879 Johann Martin Schleyer, a Catholic priest in southern Germany, published Volapük, an international language. In the 1880s it spread astonishingly fast, with clubs, journals and congresses, and supporters claimed a very large following, though such numbers are hard to verify. But Volapük was difficult, with complex grammar and words so altered from their sources that they were hard to recognise. When reformers proposed simplifying it, Schleyer insisted on keeping control, and the movement split. Within a few years most followers had moved to a rival.\n\nThat rival was Esperanto, published in 1887 by Ludwik Zamenhof, an eye doctor from Białystok, then part of the Russian Empire. He believed a neutral shared language could reduce the mistrust between the Poles, Jews, Germans and Russians around him. Spelling is phonetic and grammar has very few exceptions."],
    ["Persecuted but persistent", "Esperanto survived dictators and the rise of English, and remains the most successful international language ever created.",
      "Esperanto's internationalism made authoritarian regimes suspicious. In Nazi Germany it was attacked as a Jewish conspiracy, partly because Zamenhof was Jewish, and the movement was suppressed. In Stalin's Soviet Union, many Esperantists were arrested in the purges of the late 1930s, accused of contact with foreigners. Zamenhof's own children were killed in the Holocaust.\n\nEven so, the language survived, holding world congresses, publishing original literature and translations, and later thriving online. Estimates of speakers vary enormously, from tens of thousands of fluent users to perhaps a couple of million with some knowledge.\n\nIt never became the global second language its founder hoped for. English largely took that role, for reasons of power and economics rather than design. Still, it remains the most successful international language ever created."],
    ["Tolkien's secret vice", "Tolkien invented languages first and then built a world and mythology for them to live in.",
      "J. R. R. Tolkien was a professional philologist, an expert in the history of languages, and he invented languages from his teenage years onwards. He called the hobby his secret vice in a 1931 lecture.\n\nFor Tolkien, the languages came before the stories. He later explained that he built his mythology partly to give his languages a world and a history in which they could exist.\n\nHis two main Elvish languages were modelled on real ones he loved. Quenya drew on the sounds of Finnish, while Sindarin was influenced by Welsh. He also worked out how they had changed over thousands of years of imagined history. That depth helps explain why Middle-earth feels so ancient: its names carry the weight of invented centuries."],
    ["Klingon and Dothraki", "Film and television turned language invention into a career, with consistent grammar that fans can actually learn.",
      "In 1984 the linguist Marc Okrand developed the Klingon language for the film Star Trek III, building on a few words used earlier in the series. He deliberately gave it unusual features to sound alien, including a basic word order of object, verb, subject, which is rare among human languages. The Klingon Dictionary followed in 1985, and devoted fans have since translated works including Hamlet.\n\nLater, the linguist David J. Peterson created Dothraki and High Valyrian for the television series Game of Thrones, and languages for many other productions. These languages have consistent grammar and vocabulary, which is why fans can learn them. Some have even appeared in mainstream language learning apps, a sign of how seriously the art is now taken."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did philosophers like John Wilkins try to build?", ["A secret language for use by the church","Logical languages where words reveal meaning","A simplified form of everyday Latin","A trade language for merchants and sailors"], 1, "Their schemes sorted reality into categories, which made them brilliant but unusable."],
    [1, "Why did Volapük collapse?", ["It was banned by European governments","It was quickly replaced by English","It was hard, and its creator resisted change","It never had any printed materials"], 2, "Its complexity and Schleyer's insistence on control split the movement, and followers moved to Esperanto."],
    [2, "Why was Esperanto persecuted by dictatorships?", ["It threatened their national languages","It was spoken only by wealthy elites","Its internationalism made it seem suspicious","It was taught only in universities"], 2, "Regimes attacked Esperanto because it was international and connected people across borders."],
    [3, "What was unusual about how Tolkien worked?", ["He built his world to house his languages","He wrote the stories first and added words later","He translated his stories from Old Norse","He took his languages from older fantasy"], 0, "Tolkien built his world partly to give his invented languages a place and a history."],
    [4, "Why can fans actually learn Klingon and Dothraki?", ["They are copied from real languages","They are simple versions of English","They have only a few hundred words","They have consistent grammar and vocabulary"], 3, "Professional language creators give these languages systematic rules, so they work like real languages."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Most invented languages died young. Why a few became real communities."});

WB.L("sign-languages", "literature", "intermediate", "Sign languages: real languages, not gestures",
  "Sign languages have their own grammar, history and even accents. They are not universal, and they are not English on the hands.",
  [
    ["Myths about signing", "Sign languages are many separate natural languages with their own grammar, not universal gestures or spoken words spelled on the hands.",
      "Many hearing people assume sign language is one universal system of gestures, or a spoken language spelled out with the hands. Neither is true. There are many sign languages around the world, and one widely used reference catalogue lists well over a hundred.\n\nThey arise naturally wherever deaf people form communities, and they differ from each other as spoken languages do. British Sign Language and American Sign Language are not mutually intelligible, even though both countries speak English. Each has its own vocabulary and grammar, which are not copied from the spoken language around it.\n\nFingerspelling, where words are spelled letter by letter, exists but is used mainly for names and borrowed terms, not everyday conversation."],
    ["A French connection", "American Sign Language differs from British because it descends largely from French Sign Language through a historical accident.",
      "Why is American Sign Language so different from British? The answer is a historical accident. In 1815 Thomas Hopkins Gallaudet, an American minister, travelled to Britain to learn how to teach deaf children, but the schools he visited were secretive about their methods. Instead he met teachers from the Paris school for the deaf, which welcomed him, and studied there with Laurent Clerc, a deaf teacher.\n\nClerc agreed to come to America, and in 1817 they founded what is now the American School for the Deaf in Hartford, Connecticut. The French signs Clerc brought mixed with local sign systems used by American deaf people. The result was ASL, which still shares many signs with French Sign Language. Gallaudet University in Washington, DC, is named in his honour."],
    ["The island where everyone signed", "On Martha's Vineyard hearing and deaf people shared a sign language, suggesting deafness becomes a disability when society cannot communicate.",
      "On Martha's Vineyard, off Massachusetts, a hereditary form of deafness was unusually common from the 1700s into the early 1900s, especially in certain villages. A local sign language developed, and many hearing islanders learnt it too.\n\nDeaf people married, worked, voted and joined community life much like anyone else, and hearing residents sometimes signed to each other even when no deaf person was present. The anthropologist Nora Ellen Groce documented this history in a 1985 book whose title reports what older residents told her: that everyone there spoke sign language.\n\nThe case suggests that deafness becomes a disability largely when society cannot communicate with deaf people. As the island became less isolated, the local sign language died out."],
    ["The Milan ban", "A hearing-led congress banned sign language from deaf classrooms in 1880, yet sign languages survived through deaf communities.",
      "In 1880, an international congress of educators of the deaf met in Milan. Almost all delegates were hearing, and they voted overwhelmingly for oralism, teaching deaf children only through speech and lip-reading, and against sign language in classrooms.\n\nOver the following decades, schools in many countries followed this approach. Deaf children were often punished for signing, and some deaf teachers lost their jobs. Many deaf adults later described their schooling as deeply damaging, with years spent drilling speech instead of learning.\n\nYet sign languages survived in dormitories, playgrounds and deaf clubs, passed from child to child. In 2010 an international congress on deaf education, held in Vancouver, formally rejected the Milan resolutions and apologised for their effects."],
    ["Proof, and Auslan", "Research showed that signs have structure like spoken words, and Auslan belongs to the same family as British and New Zealand Sign Language.",
      "For a long time even scholars thought sign was mere gesture. In 1960 William Stokoe, a hearing English teacher at Gallaudet College, showed that ASL signs break into meaningful building blocks, such as handshape, location and movement, much as spoken words are built from sounds. Change one element and the meaning changes. Later work found rich grammar, including space used to track who did what to whom, and raised eyebrows marking a yes or no question.\n\nAustralia's sign language is Auslan, a name that came into wide use in the 1980s. It descends mainly from British Sign Language, with some Irish influence, and is closely related to New Zealand Sign Language. Auslan users fingerspell with two hands, while ASL uses one. It was recognised in the 1987 national language policy."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which statement about sign languages is accurate?", ["They are all mutually understandable","They copy the grammar of spoken languages","Each has its own vocabulary and grammar","They rely mainly on fingerspelling"], 2, "Sign languages arise naturally in deaf communities and differ from each other as spoken languages do."],
    [1, "Why does American Sign Language differ so much from British?", ["It was invented from scratch in Hartford","It copies English word order","It descends largely from French Sign Language","British schools refused deaf students"], 2, "Gallaudet learned from Paris teachers, and Clerc's French signs mixed with local American signs."],
    [2, "What does the Martha's Vineyard story suggest?", ["Disability often comes from social barriers","Deafness is easily cured in isolated places","Sign languages cannot survive for long","Hearing people learn signing with difficulty"], 0, "Where everyone could sign, deaf islanders took part fully in community life."],
    [3, "What did the 1880 Milan congress decide?", ["Sign language should be taught in every school","Deaf teachers should lead all classes","Schools should use fingerspelling only","Deaf children should be taught by speech alone"], 3, "The oralist ruling harmed many deaf students, though sign languages survived outside classrooms."],
    [4, "What did Stokoe's 1960 research show about ASL?", ["Signs are mostly pictures drawn in the air","Signs have parts, such as handshape and movement","Signs follow the grammar of spoken English","Signs have no consistent rules at all"], 1, "Signs break into building blocks, just as words break into sounds, so sign is a true language."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In some places everyone signed, whether or not they were deaf. How that happened."});

WB.L("how-reading-works", "literature", "intermediate", "What your eyes and brain do when you read",
  "Reading feels smooth and effortless, but your eyes are jumping, stopping and skipping all the time. And your brain is using circuits that evolved for something else.",
  [
    ["A borrowed brain", "Writing is too recent for evolution to have built a reading brain, so reading repurposes circuits that evolved for other tasks.",
      "Humans have been speaking for at least tens of thousands of years, probably far longer, but writing was invented only around 5,000 years ago, and mass literacy is barely a few centuries old. That is far too recent for evolution to have produced a brain built for reading.\n\nSo how do we do it? The neuroscientist Stanislas Dehaene proposed a theory called neuronal recycling. Learning to read repurposes circuits that evolved for other jobs, such as recognising objects and faces, and links them to the brain's language areas.\n\nIn children learning to read, scans show a region on the underside of the left hemisphere becoming increasingly tuned to written letters and words. It is often called the visual word form area. In skilled readers it responds strongly to letters in a familiar script, and much less to unfamiliar symbols."],
    ["Recognising words", "One brain region becomes specialised in recognising written words, and damage to it can leave a person able to write but unable to read.",
      "The visual word form area sits in the left fusiform gyrus, near areas that respond to faces and objects. It helps us recognise a word whether it is written in capitals, lower case or different fonts, an impressive feat of pattern recognition.\n\nDamage to this area, for example from a stroke, can cause a condition in which a person can still write but struggles to read back what they have just written.\n\nResearch also suggests that learning to read changes the brain in other ways, including how it processes spoken sounds. This partly explains why reading and phonics are so closely linked."],
    ["Eyes jump, not glide", "Your eyes read in quick jumps and pauses, and the brain stitches them into what feels like smooth reading.",
      "Watch someone's eyes as they read and you will see they do not move smoothly along the line. They make quick jumps called saccades, separated by brief pauses called fixations. In English, a typical fixation lasts roughly a quarter of a second, and a typical jump covers around seven to nine letters.\n\nDuring the jump itself, you take in almost no useful visual information. The brain stitches the pauses together so that reading feels continuous.\n\nReaders skip many short, predictable words, such as the and of, and land more often on longer content words. About one jump in ten or so goes backwards as the reader checks something. Researchers study all this with eye trackers, which record exactly where and when the eyes stop."],
    ["The window of attention", "You see only a few letters clearly at a time, and your window of attention extends ahead in your reading direction.",
      "Sharp vision covers only a tiny part of what your eyes see, so at each fixation you can identify only a handful of letters clearly. Experiments in which on-screen text changes depending on where the reader is looking have measured this window.\n\nFor English readers it extends roughly three or four letters to the left of the fixation point and about fourteen or fifteen to the right, because that is where the next words are. For readers of Hebrew, which runs right to left, the window is reversed.\n\nInformation at the edge of the window helps the brain plan the next jump and get a head start on the next word. Reading, in short, is constant prediction."],
    ["Speed reading and myths", "Reading much faster usually means understanding much less, and the famous scrambled-letters meme is not Cambridge research.",
      "Typical silent reading speeds for adults are roughly 200 to 300 words per minute. A major 2016 review led by the eye-movement scientist Keith Rayner concluded that speed reading claims of thousands of words per minute do not hold up. The limit is the brain's speed in recognising words and understanding language, not the eyes. Pushing faster means comprehension drops. Speed reading is essentially skimming, a useful skill for finding information but no shortcut to deep understanding. Faster reading comes mainly from reading more.\n\nThe scrambled-letters meme claims research at Cambridge shows letter order does not matter if the first and last letters are right. Matt Davis, a Cambridge scientist, found no such study. The text works partly because many words are short, function words are intact, and letters stay near their correct positions. Jumbled text still slows readers down."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does the brain manage to read despite writing being so recent?", ["It uses a dedicated reading organ","It copies speech centres entirely","It relies on memory alone","It repurposes circuits from other tasks"], 3, "Neuronal recycling adapts older circuits, such as those for recognising objects and faces, for reading."],
    [1, "What can damage to the visual word form area cause?", ["Losing the ability to speak","Being able to write but not read it back","Forgetting how to recognise faces","Being unable to hear sounds"], 1, "This region is specialised for recognising written words, so damage can disrupt reading while writing survives."],
    [2, "What do your eyes actually do while you read?", ["Glide smoothly along each line","Scan every letter one by one","Jump and pause, seeing little mid-jump","Fix on each word for a full second"], 2, "Saccades and fixations are stitched together by the brain so that reading feels continuous."],
    [3, "Why does the attention window reach further in your reading direction?", ["The eyes see better on one side","Letters ahead are larger","The next words to be read lie ahead","Words behind need more checking"], 2, "Information ahead helps the brain plan the next jump, so reading is constant prediction."],
    [4, "What does research say about speed reading?", ["Much faster reading usually reduces comprehension","It can double comprehension with practice","It works if you stop hearing words in your head","It is possible with special eye training"], 0, "The brain's word recognition is the limit, so very fast reading is really skimming."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your eyes never glide across a page. What they do instead may surprise you."});

WB.L("banned-books", "literature", "intermediate", "Banned books: the trials that changed what we can read",
  "Popes, customs officers and courts have all tried to stop people reading certain books. Again and again, the ban made the book more famous.",
  [
    ["The Church's list", "For four centuries the Catholic Church kept an official list of forbidden books, which grew from fear of the printing press.",
      "In 1559, under Pope Paul IV, the Catholic Church published the Index Librorum Prohibitorum, the Index of Prohibited Books. Catholics were forbidden to read or own listed works without permission.\n\nThe Index grew out of anxiety about the printing press and the Protestant Reformation, which spread new ideas faster than authorities could control them. Over time it included works by figures such as the astronomer Galileo and the philosopher René Descartes. Galileo's Dialogue, which defended the idea that the Earth moves around the Sun, stayed on the list for around two centuries.\n\nThe final edition appeared in 1948, and the Index was formally abolished in 1966, ending its legal force."],
    ["Ulysses and Lady Chatterley", "Landmark trials in 1933 and 1960 won the right to publish frank novels by judging a book as a whole and weighing its literary merit.",
      "James Joyce's Ulysses broke many rules, including frank descriptions of sex and the body. The full book appeared in Paris in 1922, then was smuggled and seized by customs. In 1933 Random House arranged a seizure to provoke a test case. Judge John M. Woolsey ruled the book, read as a whole, was not obscene, and it was published legally in the United States in 1934.\n\nD. H. Lawrence's Lady Chatterley's Lover stayed banned in Britain in full for decades. A 1959 law allowed literary merit as a defence, and Penguin tested it in 1960 with a cheap, unabridged paperback. The prosecutor asked jurors if it was a book they would want their wives or servants to read, which sounded hopelessly out of touch. The jury acquitted Penguin."],
    ["Australia's long list", "For much of the twentieth century Australia ran one of the strictest censorship regimes in the Western world, until pressure in the 1960s and 1970s forced reform.",
      "Australian censorship ran largely through customs, which seized imported books at the border, alongside state obscenity laws. For long periods the list of banned titles was itself kept confidential, so readers often did not know what they were missing.\n\nWorks by Joyce, Lawrence and many others were banned at various times, as were political works judged subversive. Lady Chatterley's Lover stayed banned in Australia for several years after the British trial.\n\nPressure for change grew through the 1960s. In 1970, Penguin Australia secretly printed Philip Roth's banned novel Portnoy's Complaint locally and distributed it, provoking prosecutions in several states. Around the same time, the federal customs minister Don Chipp began dramatically loosening the system, moving toward a classification approach."],
    ["Fire and fatwa", "Censorship has sometimes gone beyond bans into book burning and violence against authors and translators.",
      "On 10 May 1933, Nazi-organised students in Berlin and other German cities burnt tens of thousands of books by Jewish, left-wing and other authors deemed un-German. More than a century earlier the poet Heinrich Heine, whose own works were burnt, had written in a play that where books are burnt, people will eventually be burnt too.\n\nIn 1989 Iran's supreme leader, Ayatollah Khomeini, issued a fatwa calling for the death of Salman Rushdie over his novel The Satanic Verses, which many Muslims considered blasphemous. Rushdie spent years in hiding. His Japanese translator, Hitoshi Igarashi, was murdered in 1991.\n\nIn 2022, Rushdie himself was stabbed at a public lecture in New York state and lost the sight of one eye."],
    ["Why bans backfire", "Banning a book often advertises it, and today's challenges target library shelves rather than customs lists.",
      "Censors face a stubborn problem: forbidding a book advertises it. The Lady Chatterley trial turned a little-read novel into a bestseller, and Ulysses gained a glamorous reputation partly because readers had to smuggle it.\n\nToday, book bans more often take the form of challenges to books in school and public libraries, where parents or groups ask for titles to be removed. Organisations such as the American Library Association track these challenges, which in recent years have frequently targeted books about race, gender and sexuality.\n\nPeople still argue about where the line falls between protecting children and restricting ideas. Many libraries celebrate an annual Banned Books Week, inviting readers to discover the books others tried to keep from them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What prompted the Church to create the Index of Prohibited Books?", ["A shortage of paper for printing books","A dispute over translations of the Bible","A rise in book smuggling from overseas","Fear of new ideas spreading in print"], 3, "The printing press spread new ideas faster than authorities could control them."],
    [1, "What did the 1933 Ulysses ruling establish?", ["All books must be approved by customs","Books must be judged as a whole","Foreign books cannot be sold","Publishers must remove frank scenes"], 1, "Judge Woolsey found Ulysses, read as a whole, was not obscene, opening the way to publication."],
    [2, "How did Australian book censorship largely operate?", ["By local parish licences alone","Through publisher self-regulation","Customs seizures and secret lists","By boards run by universities"], 2, "Customs checked imports at the border, and readers often could not see the lists of banned titles."],
    [3, "What was the Nazi book burning aimed at?", ["Only books that attacked the Church","Foreign newspapers and magazines only","Authors the regime called un-German","Scientific and technical textbooks"], 2, "The burnings of 1933 targeted authors the regime judged un-German."],
    [4, "Why do bans often backfire?", ["A ban makes people curious about the book","Banned books are cheaper to print","Authors write more when they are censored","Libraries stock more copies of them"], 0, "A ban advertises the book, as the Lady Chatterley trial and the smuggling of Ulysses showed."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A ban meant to silence a book tends to do the opposite. Four famous cases."});

WB.L("language-shapes-thought", "literature", "advanced", "Does your language shape how you think?",
  "Do Inuit really have hundreds of words for snow? Does a language without left and right change how you see the world? The truth is stranger and more subtle than the myths.",
  [
    ["Whorf's big idea", "Language may nudge what we notice and remember, but it does not set the limits of what we can think.",
      "Benjamin Lee Whorf was a chemical engineer who worked for a fire insurance company in Connecticut and studied linguistics in his spare time, partly under Edward Sapir. He noticed workers being careless near drums labelled ‘empty’ that still held explosive vapour. The word, he suggested, was shaping their behaviour. In the 1930s he argued that speakers of very different languages think differently. This became the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, though neither man stated it formally.\n\nThe strong version says language sets the limits of thought. It is almost certainly false: babies and animals reason without words, and we keep inventing words for new ideas. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says language nudges what you notice and remember."],
    ["Two famous myths", "The Hopi no-time claim and the Inuit snow-words claim are both exaggerations that careful linguists debunked.",
      "Whorf claimed the Hopi language of Arizona had no words or grammar for time as we understand it. In 1983 the linguist Ekkehart Malotki documented extensive Hopi time expressions, including words for days, seasons and units of time.\n\nThe claim that Inuit have dozens or hundreds of words for snow grew from a modest remark by the anthropologist Franz Boas, who mentioned four root words. It swelled with each retelling until Laura Martin and Geoffrey Pullum debunked it in the 1980s. Inuit languages build long words from many parts, so counting words misleads, and English has plenty of snow words too."],
    ["No left or right", "Guugu Yimithirr speakers use compass directions instead of left and right, and keep track of direction at all times.",
      "Some of the strongest evidence for linguistic relativity comes from Australia. Speakers of Guugu Yimithirr, from Cape York in Queensland, do not use words like left, right, in front or behind to say where things are. They use cardinal directions, even at small scales: there is an ant on your northern leg.\n\nJohn Haviland and Stephen Levinson found that speakers kept remarkably accurate track of direction at all times, even indoors or after travelling, because their language demands it. When people turned around and recreated arrangements of objects, such speakers preserved compass direction. English and Dutch speakers preserved left and right."],
    ["Colour, number and time", "Language can sharpen colour distinctions, supply tools such as number words, and shape how people picture time.",
      "Russian has separate basic words for lighter blue (goluboy) and darker blue (siniy). In a 2007 study, Russian speakers were slightly faster at telling two shades apart across that boundary, but not when they silently repeated numbers, which suggests language was involved.\n\nThe Pirahã of the Amazon have no exact number words. In several experiments they struggled with exact quantities beyond about three, though results varied. Number words may be a cognitive tool for exactness.\n\nEnglish speakers lay time out left to right, Hebrew and Arabic speakers often right to left, and Kuuk Thaayorre speakers on Cape York east to west, whichever way they face. Aymara links the past with the front, since you can see it."],
    ["Where the debate stands", "Most researchers accept a modest form of linguistic relativity, with caution about the headline findings.",
      "Most researchers now accept a modest form of relativity. Language does not trap anyone's mind, but it guides attention, makes some distinctions easier to notice and remember, and provides tools like number words.\n\nCaution is still essential. Some well-publicised findings, such as grammatical gender changing how people describe objects, have proved hard to replicate. Claims linking grammar to behaviour like saving money remain contested. Critics such as the psychologist Steven Pinker argue that thought runs on something deeper than any particular language. Learning another language may not rewire your mind, but it can show you distinctions you never noticed."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is the strong version of the Sapir-Whorf idea almost certainly false?", ["Translation between languages is never possible","People can think about things they have no words for","Children learn grammar before they hear any words","Words never influence what people pay attention to"], 1, "People, babies and animals can reason without words, so language does not set the limits of thought."],
    [1, "How did the claim that Inuit have hundreds of words for snow arise?", ["Linguists counted them in a large survey","A study of Hopi time words was misapplied","A modest remark about four root words was exaggerated","Inuit grammar has a separate form for each snowfall"], 2, "The claim grew from a small observation that was inflated with every retelling."],
    [2, "Why do Guugu Yimithirr speakers keep such accurate track of direction?", ["Everyday descriptions require compass directions","They carry maps and compasses everywhere","They avoid travelling away from home","Their language has no words for place"], 0, "A language that demands compass directions trains its speakers to know them at all times."],
    [3, "What did the Pirahã number studies suggest?", ["Numbers are an inborn human sense","Number words may be a tool for exactness","People cannot learn to count as adults","Large quantities cannot be estimated at all"], 1, "Without number words, exact quantities beyond about three became hard, so number words extend what we can do."],
    [4, "What do most researchers now accept about language and thought?", ["Language guides attention without trapping the mind","Language fully determines what people can think","Language has no effect on thinking at all","Grammatical gender reliably changes perception"], 0, "A modest form of relativity holds: language nudges attention and offers tools, but it does not limit thought."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Does the word for something change how you see it? The truth is subtler than myth."});

